# 🛠 Cursor Rules – Supabase + Edge Functions

## ✅ General Code Quality

- Always follow SOLID principles (Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, Dependency Inversion).
- Ensure code is readable, modular, and maintainable.
- Add meaningful docstrings and inline comments explaining logic when needed.
- Always handle edge cases gracefully (null values, empty responses, invalid input).
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Use functional decomposition to keep functions short and focused.

## 🔒 Security & Authorization

- Always use Supabase Auth for user authentication and role-based access control.
- Never expose service keys in client code.
- All Supabase Edge Functions must validate the JWT token before executing sensitive operations.
- Check row-level security (RLS) policies in database queries.
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection.

## ⚡ Supabase Edge Functions

Edge Functions should always:

- Be small, focused, and single-purpose.
- Validate input payloads before processing.
- Return clear error messages with proper HTTP status codes.
- Log important events/errors for observability.

## 📚 Documentation & Best Practices

- Always follow Supabase official documentation when integrating features.
- When unsure, prefer Supabase-recommended libraries and approaches over custom solutions.
- Write README or inline documentation for each new function explaining:
  - Purpose
  - Input/Output
  - Dependencies

## 🧪 Testing & Reliability

- Write unit tests for all business logic.
- Mock Supabase client in tests to avoid hitting production DB.
- Ensure functions are tested for:
  - Valid input
  - Invalid input
  - Unauthorized access
  - Performance under load

## 🚀 Performance & Scalability

- Use efficient database queries (avoid N+1 queries).
- Prefer pagination for large datasets.
- Cache results when possible if data is not sensitive.